SUMMARY: criteria 3-10 all PASS with cited command evidence. Item 1 (the
mid-run oracle amendment) is cleared -- I reproduced the strictness claim
rather than taking it on trust. The FAIL is confined to ITEM 2: the single
point of failure the merge introduced is REACHABLE and SILENT, which is the
condition the brief itself defines as "a worse defect than the one this task
fixed". One line of Makefile would close it.

Environment: node v24.12.0, go1.26.6 linux/amd64, branch fix/review-aug16,
working tree uncommitted. I wrote nothing in the repo but this file; verified
with `sha256sum -c` before and after every experiment (all four watched files
OK) and `git status --short` unchanged.

=====================================================================
ITEM 1 -- THE MID-RUN ORACLE AMENDMENT: CLEARED, NOT SELF-SERVING
=====================================================================
Read `.swarm/tier3/S5/accept.sh` check 5 in full. It no longer greps the
glue's source. It overwrites
internal/handlers/accounts/testdata/js/warnings_dom_harness.js with a harness
that prints `RESULT <name> FAIL {}` for all three named checks and
`process.exit(0)`, requires `go test -run
TestSyncWarnings_ClientRegressionHarness` to FAIL, then restores.

I did NOT accept "strictly stronger" as an assertion. I constructed the
discriminating case -- a glue that PASSES the old grep while being genuinely
broken -- in a scratch copy at $SCRATCH/b2 (rsync of the repo, repo itself
untouched):

  regressed the matcher to the attempt-3 defect
    passRE := regexp.MustCompile(regexp.QuoteMeta(name))      // name-only
  and added `var wantPassToken = "PASS"` so the SOURCE carries the exact
  token the old grep demanded.

  $ grep -q 'PASS"' internal/handlers/accounts/warnings_client_regression_test.go
    -> OLD check5 (grep for PASS"): PASSES the broken glue  <-- FALSE PASS
  $ <inject RESULT..FAIL + exit 0>; go test -run TestSyncWarnings_...
    -> AMENDED check5: go test rc=0 (oracle reports FAIL -> defect CAUGHT)

So there exists a broken implementation the old grep accepts and the amended
check rejects, and (see criterion 6 below) no shape of broken glue I could
build survives the amended check. The amendment is a strict strengthening,
evidence-backed, not merely argued. It cannot have converted a broken arm into
a passing one: a name-matching arm fails the injected fixture BECAUSE the
fixture contains all three check names, which is exactly what makes it
discriminate name-matching from PASS-matching.

It also could not have changed the arm selection. The amendment touches check
5 only; wt-alt lost 6 checks, all of them check-3 Makefile-wiring items
(`.swarm/tier3/S5/report.md`, wt-alt output block), and wt-alt's check-5 line
reads PASS both before and after. Disclosure in the report, in the section
"Oracle defect found and fixed mid-run", is accurate as written. I do not
find the amendment self-serving.

ONE PROCESS DEFECT IN THE AMENDED ORACLE (not a criterion, reported as fact):
check 5 mutates a TRACKED repo file in place and restores with a plain `cp`
at the end of the branch. There is no `trap ... EXIT/INT/TERM`. `set -u` is
set but not `-e`. An interrupted or killed oracle run therefore leaves a
FORGED harness (all-FAIL, exit 0) sitting in the working tree, which a later
`make test` would then legitimately fail on -- confusing but loud -- and which
a careless `git add -A` would commit. It also makes accept.sh unsafe to run
concurrently with another checker reading the same tree. I ran it anyway per
the brief and confirmed clean restoration:
  $ sha256sum -c .../pre.sha
    web/templates/pages/accounts.html: OK
    internal/handlers/accounts/testdata/js/warnings_dom_harness.js: OK
    internal/handlers/accounts/warnings_client_regression_test.go: OK
    Makefile: OK

=====================================================================
ITEM 2 -- THE SINGLE POINT OF FAILURE: **REACHABLE AND SILENT** -> FAIL
=====================================================================
Makefile:179-180 is the whole guard:

  GOTEST_TARGETS := $(shell awk '/^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+:/{split($$0,a,":"); t=a[1]} index($$0,"$$(GO)" " test"){print t}' $(firstword $(MAKEFILE_LIST)) | sort -u)
  $(foreach t,$(GOTEST_TARGETS),$(eval $(t): check-node))

`test:` at Makefile:189 now reads `test: check-go` -- the explicit check-node
prerequisite from attempt 2 is GONE. Every target's guard is derived, and
there is NO assertion anywhere that the derivation produced anything. There
is no `$(if $(strip $(GOTEST_TARGETS)),,$(error ...))`, no Go test that
asserts the wiring, and `.swarm/tier3/S5/accept.sh` is not run by any repo
tooling (`ls .github/workflows/` -> `release.yml` only; grepping it for
`go test|make |node` returns nothing, so NOTHING in CI runs the suite at all).
The scan is the only thing standing between the developer and a silent skip,
and nothing watches the scan.

I demonstrated total, silent evaporation twice, in scratch copies:

CASE A -- someone renames the Go variable (`sed 's/$(GO) test/$(GOCMD) test/g'`):
  $ make -pn | grep -E "^(test|test-unit|race|fuzz|test-coverage|test-integration):"
    test-coverage: check-go
    test-integration: check-go
    fuzz: check-go
    test-unit: check-go
    race: check-go
    test: check-go
  ALL SIX guards gone. `make -n test` -> prints the test command, rc=0, and
  make emits not one word of diagnostic.

CASE B -- the go-test targets move into an included fragment
(`Makefile` lines 189-228 moved to `mk/test.mk`, `include mk/test.mk`):
  $ make -pn | grep -E "^(test|test-unit|race|fuzz|test-coverage|test-integration):"
    test-coverage: check-go   test-integration: check-go   fuzz: check-go
    test-unit: check-go       race: check-go               test: check-go
  ALL SIX guards gone, because the scan reads only
  `$(firstword $(MAKEFILE_LIST))`. `make -n test-unit` prints
  `/home/darrell/go-sdk/go/bin/go test -v ./internal/...`, rc=0, no warning.

In both cases the repo returns EXACTLY to the attempt-2 defect -- `make test`
green with the harness silently skipped -- with no signal of any kind. This
is the failure mode the brief names: "A guard that evaporates without a sound
is a worse defect than the one this task fixed." It is reachable by ordinary
maintenance edits, it fails quietly, and it now takes all six targets at once
because the explicit prerequisite floor was removed.

WHAT IS MISSING (precise): an assertion, adjacent to the scan, that the scan
found something -- e.g.
  ifeq ($(strip $(GOTEST_TARGETS)),)
  $(error GOTEST_TARGETS scan matched no targets: the check-node guard has
          silently evaporated. See Makefile:158-180.)
  endif
That converts every scenario above from silent regression into a hard,
immediate make error. It costs three lines and cannot regress anything.
(A stronger form would also cross-check the derived set against a
`make -pn`-derived list, but the emptiness guard alone closes the total-loss
case, which is the one that matters.)

