(5) FUTURE TARGETS -- claim CONFIRMED; uncovered constructions found and listed
Scratch copy of Makefile, three targets appended by hand, none wired:
  $ make -pn | grep '^bench-integration:'  -> bench-integration: check-node
  $ make -pn | grep '^covered-later:'      -> covered-later: check-go check-node
  $ make -pn | grep '^smoke-legacy:'       -> smoke-legacy:            [NOT covered]
So a future target written in the house idiom
(`\t$(GO) test -bench=. ./internal/...`) IS covered with nobody remembering
to wire it. The claim in Makefile:169-178 and in
warnings_client_regression_test.go:18-23 is true as stated.

Plausible targets that are NOT covered (all silent):
  a) bare `go test ./...` in the recipe (smoke-legacy above) -- the single
     most likely way a new contributor writes it; `go` is on PATH anyway.
  b) `$(GO)  test ./...` (two spaces) -> `double-space:` with no prereq.
     Cosmetic whitespace defeats the guard.
  c) a recipe that expands a `define`d variable:
       define RUN_TESTS
       \t$(GO) test ./...
       endef
       uses-define:
       \t$(RUN_TESTS)
     -> `uses-define:` with NO prereq. Worse, the define's body line was
     attributed to the PRECEDING target header, so an unrelated target
     (`gotestsum-target`) wrongly acquired check-node:
       GOTEST_TARGETS := fuzz gotestsum-target race test test-coverage test-integration test-unit
       gotestsum-target: check-node        <- false positive
       uses-define:                        <- false negative
     Both errors at once from one construct. This is the same misattribution
     class as the `clean` trap in item 7, generalised: ANY non-recipe line
     containing `$(GO) test` tags the previous target header and hides the
     real one.
  d) `gotestsum`, `$(GO) -C dir test`, or a renamed variable -- none match.
  e) the include/rename cases in item 2 above, which take everything.

