(7) SELF-REFERENCE TRAP -- PASS
  $ grep -n 'GOTEST_TARGETS :=' Makefile | grep -c '$(GO) test'   -> 0
  The scanner writes `"$$(GO)" " test"` (two concatenated awk literals), so
  the line does not contain the token it searches for. The comment at
  Makefile:177-178 documents exactly this.
  $ make -pn | grep -E "^GOTEST_TARGETS"
    GOTEST_TARGETS := fuzz race test test-coverage test-integration test-unit
  $ make -pn | grep -E "^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+:" | grep -w check-node
    fuzz: check-go check-node        race: check-go check-node
    test: check-go check-node        test-coverage: check-go check-node
    test-integration: check-go check-node   test-unit: check-go check-node
    (.PHONY line also lists check-node, as a name, correctly not a prereq)
  Prerequisites of the targets that must NOT have acquired node:
    clean:            (none)          build: check-go
    vet: check-go                     dev:   check-go
    all: build   run: build   fmt: check-go   tidy: check-go
    deps: check-go   static:  (none)  vuln:  (none)
    install-go: (none)   help: (none)
  None carries check-node. Confirmed.
  I also proved the trap is REAL and the mitigation load-bearing: collapsing
  the concatenation back to `index($$0,"$$(GO) test")` in a scratch copy gives
    GOTEST_TARGETS := clean fuzz race test test-coverage test-integration test-unit
    clean: check-node
  i.e. `make clean` would then need node. That failure at least is LOUD --
  unlike the item-2 failure -- but it means the odd-looking string
  concatenation must never be "tidied up" by a formatter or a helpful editor.

